*************************************************************************	 Copyright (C) 1989,1991*	 Apple Computer, Inc.*	 All Rights Reserved************************************************************************** REVISION HISTORY** 2-Dec-91	Dave Lyons** For 6.0d60, split this file out of QDAux.asm so PixelMap2Rgn can* be a dynamic segment.************************************************************************	include 'all.macros'	include 'special.macros'	include 'e16.memory'; offset constants for LocInfooportSCB	equ 0optrToPixImage	equ 2owidth	equ 6oboundsRect	equ 8************************************************************************* pixelMap2Rgn function** NOTE:  This code is Apple property and should not be made available*        to anyone outside Apple Computer, Inc.*        This code should be distributed within Apple on a*        need-to-know basis.** Copyright (C)* Apple Computer, Inc.  1989* All Rights Reserved.** Written by Eric Soldan.** This function generates a region from a pixelmap.** This is the previous definition of a region that was published.*   00:  word:  length of region*   02:  rect:  bounding rectangle of region*   0A:  data:  if the region is not empty or rectangular, the rest of*               the information was here, in some cryptic format.** A region is basically a compact way to describe an arbitrary area.* Even more importantly, it is a way that can be decoded and used fast.* Also, it is efficient for the types of areas commonly encountered* when using windows and the toolBox in general.** The bounding rectangle portion of a region is simply that.  It is the* smallest rectangle that contains all the points that are inside the* region (described by the rest of the data, if any).  The length word* is the total size of the region (length word, bounding rect, and all* data).  That is a quick overview of the region data structure.  Now,* to describe the data portion completely:** Let's say that the below diagram is the outline of a region.  The* lower case letters are y-coordinates.  The upper case letters are* x-coordinates. It is important to understand what is inside the* region and what is outside. In order to understandthis, note that* regions are interpreted from left to right and from top to bottom.* The lines bordering the region may be thought of as entering or exiting* edge lines, since the lines are encountered either entering or exiting* the region according to the left-to-right, top-to-bottom defintion of* regions. Because coordinates refer to the upper-left corner of pixels,* the points in entering edge lines are included in the region they border,* and exiting edge lines are excluded from the region they border. In the* diagram below the entering edge lines are represented  with asterisks,* and the exiting edge lines are represented with periods.  Again, the* asterisks are points included in the region and the periods are points* excluded from the region.**   row a                A***************B*       b                *               .*       c                *    C....D     .*       d       E********F    .    *     G******H*       e       *             I****J            .*       f       *                               .*       g       *                K..............L*       h       *                .*       i       M................N** Let's look at how the x-coordinates are represented first.  X-coordinate* data is represented in x-start/x-stop pairs.  For the first row of* the region, any pixels between x-start (inclusive) and x-stop (exclusive)* are in the region.  For the second row of the region, we have x-start/* x-stop pairs for any DIFFERENCES from the borders in the first row.  If* there are no differences between the first and second row, then we* don't need to store anything for the second row.  We can make this* description more consistent, to describe the first row as well.  First,* region data is row oriented.  Second, the row data stored is the* difference between the actual row and the row above, if any.  For the* first row, we can have a logical row above the first row that holds* nothing.  This makes sense, since the row above the first row in the* region is outside the region, and therefore holds nothing.** So, in summary, for any given row of the region, the x-coordinate* start/stop pairs represent changes from the previous row.** The data that would have to be stored would be where all of the changes* are.  This would look like:  (length)(boundingRect)(aABcCDdEFGHeIJgKLiMN)* This is rather compact.** One tricky thing is that you can have multiple x-start/x-stop pairs per* row.  This means that you have to have some kind of indicator as to how* many pairs you have per row, or where they stop.  The "where they stop"* is the one used.  An x-start value of $3FFF indicates that there are no* more x-start/x-stop pairs for the current row, and a new row is starting.** $4000 is outside the conceptual drawing space, whereas $3FFF is inside.* However, we can use $3FFF as a flag since an x-start/x-stop pair always* includes at least one pixel, and that would mean that the x-stop value* would be $4000 (for an x-start of $3FFF), and that is outside the* conceptual drawing space.  This actually means that the conceptual* drawing space does NOT include $3FFF as an x-coordinate, as far as* regions are concerned.  So, $3FFF is what we will use to start the next* row.  The word following a $3FFF will be the next row of interest, and* that will be followed by more x-start/x-stop pairs.  This goes on* and on, as you might imagine.** So, the region data (each element being a word) would look something like:*   (row data is of the form:  new row marker, y, x-start, x-stop)*   $3FFF,a,A,B,*   $3FFF,c,C,D,*   $3FFF,d,E,F,G,H,*   $3FFF,e,I,J,*   $3FFF,g,K,L,*   $3FFF,i,M,N** Remember the lower case letters are y-coordinates and the upper case* letters are x-coordinates.  To simplify even further, we don't need to* store the starting $3FFF.  We know we will start with a row value, so* there is no need for the first new row marker. ** There is just one thing to add:  The end-of-region marker.  The end of* the region is indicated by a $3FFF (new row), followed by another $3FFF.* A new row of $3FFF indicates the end of the region.  This means that a* y-coordinate of $3FFF is also not valid within a region.* * So, the entire region would look like:*         length:  64*   boundingRect:  a,E,9,H*           data:  a,A,B,*                  $3FFF,c,C,D,*                  $3FFF,d,E,F,G,H,*                  $3FFF,e,I,J,*                  $3FFF,g,K,L,*                  $3FFF,i,M,N*                  $3FFF,$3FFF** So, given an understanding of a region data structure, here goes:* THEORY:*      This function turns a designated color-set of a pixelmap into*      a region.  The color separation is done in two stages.  First, a*      table of bit values to be kept is built.  Then, this table is*      used in a routine called processRow.  This routine doesn't actually*      xor the actual values of a row, but uses the actual value to*      index into the table of acceptable colors.  This derived value*      has 1's in the pixels that should be included in the region, and*      0's for pixels that should be excluded.  This way, the*      application can choose any color or colors to be included or*      excluded.* Setup:*      1) Build the colors-to-include-in-region table.*      2) Get the relavent information on the bitmap.*      3) Create an initial empty region.*      4) Make sure the region handle is big enough for the first*         row of region data.  Call growRgn to do this, which also*         re-dereferences it.*      5) Make a handle of zero's (zerosHndl) to use against the first*         and last rows when xor'ing with 'previous' row.*      6) Calculate the column offsets and left/right masks for*         the bitmap.*      7) Calculate the offset into the bitmap for the first row and*         dereference the zerosHndl.* Outer loop (nextRow):*      Loop for as many rows as there are.  Call processRow per row.*      Call growRgn after each processRow call to make sure that there*      is enough space to add the next row.  This frees up processRow*      from having to make sure that the region is big enough to hold*      the data that is being added to it.  Lots fewer SetHandleSize's*      this way.* Inner loop (processRow):*      The inner loop 'knows' whether each point on the row is either*      in the region or not.  It does this by keeping a state as to*      whether or not we are in the region.  (We aren't when we start*      a row, since anything to the left of the row is by definition*      outside the region.)  It then determines transition points by*      xor'ing with the row above.  (If it is the first or last row,*      then a row of 0's is used as the other row.)  The processing*      is done a byte at a time to speed things up.  The byte for the*      row is loaded, then used as an index into a table of colors*      to be included in the region.  This table turns all pixels of*      a correct color into on pixels, and all other pixels to off*      pixels.  This is also done with the previous row.  Then the*      two derived bytes are xor'ed together.  If the result was*      non-zero, there is at least one transition in the byte, and*      some data has to be added to the region.  At that point, the*      addToRgn routine is called.  This is a big function that has*      special cases for the different possible transitions within*      one byte.  In 320 mode there are only 3.  In 640 mode there*      are 15.  If there an even number of transitions in the byte,*      then we leave the byte in the same state (either inside or*      outside the region) that we entered the byte.  If there are*      an odd number of transitions, then we leave the byte in the*      opposite state we were in.  The state indicator is either*      a $00 (outside region) or $FF (inside region).  This is very*      handy because we can then take (row.byte xor row-1.byte) and*      xor it with the state indicator.  If the result is zero, then*      there are no transitions, period.  If the result is non-zero,*      the transitions are in the same place, whether or not we are*      inside the region.  So, any of the special case routines for*      transitions in a byte that have an odd number of transitions*      (and those routines know who they are) simply xor the state*      value with $FF to change the state, and everything else*      takes care of itself.* Completion:*      Use the last row to 'close' the region.  Store the bounding*      rectangle and the region size in the first 10 bytes of the*      region.  Resize the region to the correct size.  Handle*      special case of empty region.  When all is well, clean up*      and return the region handle.*** Input:*      long:  Pointer to a locInfo structure for the pixelmap.*             The pixelmap must be locked down, if it is in a handle.*      word:  Value indicating 2-bit or 4-bit pixels (2 or 4 passed).*             This is independent of 320 or 640 mode.   This way, we*             can handle dithered or non-dithered palettes.*             320 or 640 mode is determined by the SCB in the locInfo*             for the bitmap.*      word:  color table indicating which colors to include.*             bit 0 set:  include color 0 in region.*             bit 1 set:  include color 1 in region.*             .*             .*             .*             (For 2-bit colors, only bits 0 thru 3 are used)* Output:*      This function returns a region containing the relavent pixels.*      This is true only if:*           1)  The region does not exceed 65535 bytes.*           2)  There was enough memory to construct the region.*      Possible errors:*           1)  Region too complex.*           2)  Out-of-memory.************************************************************************	longi on	longa on*********************************************************************************************************************************************** rgn = pixelMap2Rgn(locInfoPtr, mode, whichColors)** locInfoPtr:	pixelmap to convert* mode:	2-bit pixels or 4-bit pixels* whichColors:	16 bits of data indicating which colors to include.PixelMap2Rgn	proc export	DefineStacktop	wordleft	wordbottom	wordright	wordleftint	word	;Make sure these are supported.pmLeftmask	wordrightint	wordpmRightmask	wordminy	wordpmMinx	wordmaxy	wordpmMaxx	wordrow	wordpmWidth	wordrgnSize	wordrgnSize2	wordresMode	wordoldHndlSize	wordstate	wordpmTemp	wordcolorNum	wordnumRows	wordnumInRow	wordrowPtr	longprevRowPtr	longzerosHndl	longpmRgnPtr	longpmapUserID	word	;added 19-Jan-91 DALsizeLocals	EndLocalssaveDPage	wordsaveDBank	bytereturnAddr	block 6	;was 3 before I made it a tool (DAL)whichColors	word	;Which colors to do.pmMode	word	;2-bit or 4-bit pixels (2 or 4 for a value)locInfoPtr	long	;Passed parameterrgnHndl	long	;Result.********************	tax	;keep WAP in X for a while	phb	;Save and set dataBank register.	phk	plb	phd	;Save directPage register.	tsc	;Make space for locals.	sec	sbc #sizeLocals	tcs	tcd	;Set directPage register.	lda >$00000A,x	;get QD user ID (19-Jan-91 DAL)	sta <pmapUserID	jsr setup	;Prepare everything.	lda left	cmp right	bcs closeRgn	;Empty region.	dec a	sta row	;Do it from the top.********************nextRow	inc row	;Advance to next row.	lda row	cmp bottom	bcs closeRgn	;All done, so close rgn and get out of here.	jsr processRow	;Store x-pairs for this row into region.	jsr growRgn	;Get ready for next row of data.	bcs exit	;Couldn't grow the region.	lda rowPtr	;Move current row pointer to previous row pointer,	ldx rowPtr+2	;and then advance current row pointer.	sta prevRowPtr	stx prevRowPtr+2	adc pmWidth	sta rowPtr	bcc nextRow	inc rowPtr+2	bra nextRow********************* if rgnSize is still 8, then we found no data for the region.* So, it is an empty region.** A region with a rgnSize of 16 at this point is a rectangular region.  Reduce this* to a 10-byte rectangular region.  (16 because the region isn't closed yet.)* So, a region that looks like the below is a rectangular region:*  8: 3FFF    New row mark.  This will be covered by the bounding rect.* 10: miny    Beginning row of region data.* 12: pmMinx    x-pair start point.* 14: pmMaxx    x-pair stop point.* 16: ----closeRgn	lda rgnSize	;See if we have an empty region.	cmp #8	beq @emptyRgn	;We do, so make it really empty.	cmp #16	;See if we have a rectangular region.	beq @rectRgn	;We do, so just store the bounding rect.* Closing a regular region is a little tricky.  Here's the scoop:* We want to include the last row of data to close the region.  Whatever the last row* is in the pixelmap that has anything in it, that is the row we want to include* 'as is'.  So, call calcRowPtrs with the last row with anything it it.  It will set* rowPtr to this, and prevRowPtr to point to 0's.  This is exactly what we want.  When we* process the row, it will xor these two together (one being 0's), thus giving the exact* content of the last row.	lda bottom	;Close the region using the last row.  If there is	sta row	;nothing in the last row, then the region is already	dec a	;closed.  In that case processRow will do nothing.	jsr calcRowPtrs	;Point to this 'last row'.	jsr processRow	;Process the data pointed at as 'row'.*** 23-Jun-91 DAL*** There is one more rectangular case.  If our region is only 24 bytes after we've made*** sure it's closed, then there was only one pair of transitions in, and one pair to close*** it out (at some point -before- we hit the bottom of the bounds rectangle).  So we just*** set the size to 10.	ldy rgnSize	cpy #24	beq @isRectangular2*** end of 23-Jun-91 DAL; already set	ldy rgnSize	;End the region with the end-of-region marker.	lda #$3FFF	;This marker is a new row of $3FFF.	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	;NOTE:  maxy is always set to row by processRow,	iny	;if there was any data in that row.  There is	iny	;data in this row -- that is why it is our	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	;last row.  So, processRow will update maxy to	iny	;be the same as row, which was incremented past	iny	;the last row with data.  All is good.	sty rgnSize	bra @regularRgn@emptyRgn	stz miny	;Bounding rect is all 0's for an empty region.	stz bottom	;bottom is used for maxy, due to rectangular	stz pmMinx	;region not having this current.	stz pmMaxx@rectRgn	lda bottom	;maxy is not correct for rectangular region.	sta maxy	;Now it is.@isRectangular2	lda #10	;min's and max'es are all correct for	sta rgnSize	;rectangular regions at this point.@regularRgn	ldy #2	;Store bounding rectangle into region.	lda miny	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	lda pmMinx	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	lda maxy	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	lda pmMaxx	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	lda rgnSize	;Store region size in region.	sta [pmRgnPtr]	pea 0	;Fix handle size.	pha	pei rgnHndl+2	pei rgnHndl	_SetHandleSize	;Shrink handle to right size.********************exit*****	php	;Keep error status.	pha	;Keep error code.	bcc @a	;There was no error.  We succeeded.	lda  rgnHndl+1	beq @a	;We fail to even make a region.	pei rgnHndl+2	;We don't have a completed region, so dispose of it.	pei rgnHndl	_DisposeHandle	stz rgnHndl	stz rgnHndl+2@a	lda zerosHndl+1	;Get rid of work space.	beq @b	pei zerosHndl+2	pei zerosHndl	_DisposeHandle***@b	ply	;Keep error code.***	plp@b	plx	;get error code in X***	ror a***	tax	;Keep error status.	tsc	;Get rid of local variables.	clc	adc #sizeLocals	tcs	pld	;Restore directPage register.	plb	;Restore dataBank.***	lda 1,s	;Move return address.***	sta 9,s***	lda 2,s***	sta 10,s******	pla	;Get rid of passed parameters.***	pla***	pla***	pla******	txa	;Restore error status.***	rol a***	tya	;Restore error code.******	rtl	;Exit.	ldy #8	;get rid of 8 bytes of parameters	jml $e10184	;ToStrip****************************************processRow	ldy rgnSize	;Pre-store the row info.  If any column info	lda #$3FFF	;is found, rgnSize and rgnSize2 will differ by	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	;more than 4.  If they do, then store rgnSize2	iny	;in rgnSize, thus preserving the row info.	iny	lda row	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	cpy #12	bne @a	sta miny@a	lda #0	;This sets hi-byte to 0.  Important, since we are	sta state	;going to use an 8-bit acc and 16-bit indexes.	shortm	;Hi-byte of acc is 0.	ldy leftint	;Do the left byte first, so the middle bytes don't	lda [rowPtr],y	;have to be clipped conditionally to the left and	tax	;right masks.  (Hi-byte is 0)	lda convTbl,x	;Convert only color bits into 1's.	sta pmTemp*** 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda <prevRowPtr+1	ora <prevRowPtr+2	beq @doingFirstRow*** end of 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda [prevRowPtr],y	tax	;(Hi-byte is 0)	lda convTbl,x	;Convert only color bits into 1's.@doingFirstRow	eor pmTemp	;Any 1's are changes to the region.	and pmLeftMask	eor state	beq @aa	jsr addToRgn@aa	iny	;Do byte just after left edge, if there is one.	cpy rightint	;1 less than rightint, (unless rightint is 0).	bcs @c	;Go and check the right edge now.@b	lda [rowPtr],y	tax	lda convTbl,x	;Make only color bits to keep 1's.	sta pmTemp*** 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda <prevRowPtr+1	ora <prevRowPtr+2	beq @doingFirstRow2*** end of 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda [prevRowPtr],y	tax	lda convTbl,x	;Make only color bits to keep 1's.@doingFirstRow2	eor pmTemp	;The correct colored pixels that change	eor state	beq @aa	jsr addToRgn	iny	cpy rightint	bcc @b@c	bne @closeRow	;yreg > rightint	lda [rowPtr],y	tax	lda convTbl,x	;Make only color bits to keep 1's.	sta pmTemp*** 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda <prevRowPtr+1	ora <prevRowPtr+2	beq @doingFirstRow3*** end of 5-Apr-91 DAL	lda [prevRowPtr],y	tax	lda convTbl,x	;Make only color bits to keep 1's.@doingFirstRow3	eor pmTemp	;The correct colored pixels that change	and pmRightMask	eor state	beq @closeRow	jsr addToRgn@closeRow	longm	lda state	beq @d	;Row 'closed' properly.	ldy rgnSize2	lda right	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2@d	ldy rgnSize	iny	iny	iny	iny	cpy rgnSize2	beq @rts	;rgnSize and rgnSize2 differ by 4, empty row.	lda row	;This row had some data, so remember it as the	sta maxy	;maxy so far.	lda [pmRgnPtr],y	;Fetch the first x-coord for this row.	cmp pmMinx	;Check it against the running minimum x-coord.	bpl @e	sta pmMinx	;New minimum.@e	ldy rgnSize2	sty rgnSize	dey	dey	lda [pmRgnPtr],y	;Fetch the last x-coord for this row.	cmp pmMaxx	;Check it against the running maximum x-coord.	bmi @rts	sta pmMaxx	;New maximum.@rts	rts	longa offaddToRgn	bit resMode	;Always called with short acc.	bmi @add640	tax	bpl @a01	lsr a	bcs @a11@a10	tyx	;11110000	ldy rgnSize2	;^   ^	longm	txa	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm@rts	rts@a11	tyx	;11111111	ldy rgnSize2	;^	longm	txa	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rts@a01	tyx	;00001111	ldy rgnSize2	;    ^	longm	txa	asl a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state@rts0	rts@add640	asl a	tax	;Okay to lose bit 7, since it is the same as 6.	jmp (addTbl,x)addTbl	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0001100,z0001100,z0001100,z0001111	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0001100,z0001100,z0001100,z0001111	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0000000,z0000000,z0000000,z0000011	dc.w z0001100,z0001100,z0001100,z0001111	dc.w z0110000,z0110000,z0110000,z0110011	dc.w z0110000,z0110000,z0110000,z0110011	dc.w z0110000,z0110000,z0110000,z0110011	dc.w z0111100,z0111100,z0111100,z0111111	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1001100,z1001100,z1001100,z1001111	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1001100,z1001100,z1001100,z1001111	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1000000,z1000000,z1000000,z1000011	dc.w z1001100,z1001100,z1001100,z1001111	dc.w z1110000,z1110000,z1110000,z1110011	dc.w z1110000,z1110000,z1110000,z1110011	dc.w z1110000,z1110000,z1110000,z1110011	dc.w z1111100,z1111100,z1111100,z1111111z0000000	rtsz0000011	tyx	;00000011	ldy rgnSize2	;      ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz0001100	tyx	;00001100	ldy rgnSize2	;    ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz0001111	tyx	;00001111	ldy rgnSize2	;    ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz0110000	tyx	;00110000	ldy rgnSize2	;  ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz0110011	tyx	;00110011	ldy rgnSize2	;  ^ ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz0111100	tyx	;00111100	ldy rgnSize2	;  ^   ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz0111111	tyx	;00111111	ldy rgnSize2	;  ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz1000000	tyx	;11000000	ldy rgnSize2	;^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz1000011	tyx	;11000011	ldy rgnSize2	;^ ^   ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz1001100	tyx	;11001100	ldy rgnSize2	;^ ^ ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz1001111	tyx	;11001111	ldy rgnSize2	;^ ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz1110000	tyx	;11110000	ldy rgnSize2	;^   ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz1110011	tyx	;11110011	ldy rgnSize2	;^   ^ ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rtsz1111100	tyx	;11111100	ldy rgnSize2	;^     ^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	inc a	inc a	inc a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda #0	;Make sure hi-byte of acc is 0.	shortm	rtsz1111111	tyx	;11111111	ldy rgnSize2	;^	longm	txa	asl a	asl a	sta [pmRgnPtr],y	iny	iny	sty rgnSize2	txy	lda state	;Hi-byte of acc is 0 again.	shortm	eor #$FF	sta state	rts	longa on********************growRgn	lda pmWidth	;Calc how much bigger region has to get, worst case.	asl a	asl a	bit resMode	;About quadruple the width for 320 mode.	bpl @a	asl a	;About 8 times the width for 640 mode.@a	clc	adc #8	;Plus 8 more for row info (and closing mark info).	adc rgnSize	;Plus how big it already is.	bcc @b	;It isn't too big yet.	lda #$0433	;rgnFull error.	bra @err@b	ora #$03FF	;Round up so fewer mem-moves occur.	cmp oldHndlSize	clc	beq @err	sta oldHndlSize	pea 0	pha	pei rgnHndl+2	pei rgnHndl	_SetHandleSize	bcs @err	ldy #2	;Handle may have moved.	lda [rgnHndl],y	sta pmRgnPtr+2	lda [rgnHndl]	sta pmRgnPtr@err	rts********************setup	jsr calcConvTbl	;Calculate the conversion table.	lda #8	;rgnSize of 8 indicates nothing added yet.	sta rgnSize	lda #16383	;Initial 'min' is huge.	sta pmMinx	lda #-16383	;Initial 'max' is tiny.	sta pmMaxx	ldy #owidth	;Get the width and the bounding rectangle.	lda [locInfoPtr],y	sta pmWidth	iny	iny	lda [locInfoPtr],y	sta top	iny	iny	lda [locInfoPtr],y	sta left	iny	iny	lda [locInfoPtr],y	sta bottom	iny	iny	lda [locInfoPtr],y	sta right;	ldy #oportSCB;	lda [locInfoPtr],y;	xba	;Move bit 7 to bit 15.;	ora [locInfoPtr],y	;This way, it can be used as a flag, 8 or 16 bit.;	sta resMode*** 5-Apr-91 DAL -- oPortSCB is zero; don't assume bits other than 7 are unused	lda [<locInfoPtr]	and #$0080	pha	xba	ora 1,s	sta <resMode	pla*** end of 5-Apr-91 DAL	stz rgnHndl	;Flag handles as not existing yet.	stz rgnHndl+2	stz oldHndlSize	stz zerosHndl	stz zerosHndl+2	pha	;Make a region to return.	pha	_NewRgn	plx	ply	bcc @a@err	ply	;Pop a return address.	brl exit	;Exit with an error.@a	stx rgnHndl	sty rgnHndl+2	jsr growRgn	;If it can't grow, it jmps to exit with an error.	bcs @err;	pha	;Result space.;	pha;	pea 0	;Size zerosHndl big enough.;	lda pmWidth;	inc a	;Allow for slop of a word.;	inc a;	pha	;Low word of size.;	pei <pmapUserID	;19-Jan-91 DAL (was MMStartUp);	lda #0;	pha	;0 for attributes;	pha	;NULL for location.;	pha;	_NewHandle;	plx;	ply;	bcc @b;	ply	;Pop a return address.;	brl exit	;Exit with an error.;@b	stx zerosHndl;	sty zerosHndl+2;;	ldy #2	;Fill zerosHndl with 0's.  This is used for the;	lda [zerosHndl],y	;special cases of the first row and the last row.;	sta prevRowPtr+2	;Neither of these cases xor with anything else, so;	lda [zerosHndl]	;we will xor them with 0's, so the main code doesn't;	sta prevRowPtr	;have to have special cases in it.;	lda pmWidth;	lsr a;	asl a;	tay;	lda #0;@c	sta [prevRowPtr],y;	dey;	dey;	bpl @c	lda left	;Calculate int's and frac's	bit resMode	bmi @d	asl a	;Guarantees bit 0 always off for 320 mode.@d	tay	and #3	tax	tya	lsr a	lsr a	sta leftint	lda @leftmasks,x	sta pmLeftMask	lda right	bit resMode	bmi @e	asl a@e	tay	and #3	tax	tya	lsr a	lsr a	sta rightint	lda @rightmasks,x	ldx leftint	cpx rightint	bne @f	and pmLeftMask	sta pmLeftMask@f	sta pmRightMask	lda top	bra calcRowPtrs	;Uses value in acc for row to point to.@leftmasks	dc.b $FF,$3F,$0F,$03@rightmasks	dc.b $00,$C0,$F0,$FC********************calcRowPtrs	pha	;Result space.	pha	pha	;Row to point to in pixelmap.	pei pmWidth	;Width (in bytes) of pixelmap.	_Multiply	;Gives offset to top row.	ldy #optrToPixImage	;Add that to pointer to pixelmap.	pla	clc	adc [locInfoPtr],y	sta rowPtr	iny	iny	pla	adc [locInfoPtr],y	sta rowPtr+2;	ldy #2;	lda [zerosHndl],y;	sta prevRowPtr+2;	lda [zerosHndl];	sta prevRowPtr	stz prevRowPtr	stz prevRowPtr+2	rts********************calcConvTbl	ldy #254	;First, zero them all.	lda #0@a	sta convTbl,y	dey	dey	bpl @a	lda pmMode	;320 or 640 mode, which is it?	cmp #2	beq m640* In 320 mode, we need to do two passes.  The first pass is the hi-nibble pass.* 16 bytes in a row will have the hi-nibble the correct color.  We can do these* a word at a time, since they are in a row.  We just take the color number and* multiply it by 16.  This is the byte number in the table where the hi-nibbles* are to be set.  Then we just do 16 bytes in a row from there.  The second pass* is for lo-nibble colors.  This is a little more difficult.  We start at the* byte that is the color.  This is obviously the first lo-nibble involved.  From* there, we just skip 16 bytes, and that is the next involved.  We can't do* words at a time, since the involved bytes are separated by 16.  So, we just* loop 16 times doing individual bytes, and that will do it for one color.  For* all colors, we just loop for all 16 colors.  We don't do anything for colors* that aren't included, since we started by zeroing them all.  Colors that* aren't included should be zero.m320	ldx #$FFFF	;Do 16 colors, starting with 0.@a	inx	lsr whichColors	;See if color bit N is on.	bcs @b	;Yes, so do at least this one.	bne @a	;No, but there are others.	rts	;There are no more to do.@b	txa	;Set 16 in a row for the range of numbers	asl a	;that has the right hi-nibble.	asl a	asl a	asl a	;Color number * 16.	tay	;Index to first hi-nibble range.	phx	;Keep the x-reg.	ldx #8	;Do 8 words (16 bytes).@c	lda convTbl,y	;Involve these hi-nibbles.	ora #$F0F0	sta convTbl,y	;They are now involved.	iny	iny	dex	bne @c	plx	;Done with this loop.	phx	;Do all the lo-nibble guys now.	txy	ldx #16	;We can only do a byte at a time, so loop 16 times.@d	lda convTbl,y	ora #$000F	;Involve the lo-nibble.	sta convTbl,y	tya	clc	adc #16	tay	;The next one is 16 away.	dex	bne @d	;And there is a next one, so go do it.	plx	bra @a******* In 640 mode, we need to do even more.  What to do in 640 mode is table driven.* It is slower than 320 mode, but the simplicity is worth it.* Algorithm works as follows:*   There will be a first byte affected -- some byte has to be first.  This*   byte offset is calculated by taking the color affected and multiplying*   by a value.  This value depends on which bits we are affecting.  The following*   table describes it:*     bits 0-1:  multiply color by 0*     bits 2-3:  multiply color by 4*     bits 4-5:  multiply color by 16*     bits 6-7:  multiply color by 64*   We multiply by left-shifting.  So, the left-shift values would be 0,2,4,6*   There are some number of rows, depending on which bits we are affecting:*     bits 0-1:  64 rows of 1 byte.*     bits 2-3:  16 rows of 4 bytes.*     bits 4-5:  4 rows of 16 bytes.*     bits 6-7:  1 row of 64 bytes.* We will use tables like this (plus a few more) to control the looping and indexing.m640	lda whichColors	and #$0F	sta whichColors	;Make sure there aren't any extra bits on.	stz colorNum	;Start with color 0.@a	lsr whichColors	;See if color bit N is on.	bcs @b	;Yes, so do at least this one.	bne @nxtclr	;No, but there are others.	rts	;There are no more to do.@b	ldx #6	;There will be 4 passes (6, 4, 2, 0).@c	txy	;# of times to shift color to make starting index.	lda colorNum@d	dey	bmi @e	asl a	bra @d@e	tay	;This is starting index into convTbl.	lda @numRows,x	sta numRows	;Number of rows to do this pass.@f	lda @numInRow,x	sta numInRow	;Number to do in this row.@g	lda convTbl,y	ora @mask,x	;Turn on certain bits for this pass.	sta convTbl,y	iny	dec numInRow	bne @g	;More to do for this row.	tya	clc	adc @numToSkip,x	;Skip to the next row.	tay	dec numRows	bne @f	;More rows to do.	dex	dex	bpl @c	;More passes to do.@nxtclr	inc colorNum	;Advance to the next color.	bra @a	;Do more colors if we have them.@numRows	dc.w	64,16,4,1@numInRow	dc.w	1,4,16,64@mask	dc.w	$03,$0C,$30,$C0@numToSkip	dc.w	3,12,48,192convTbl	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $3C,$3C,$3C,$3F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $3C,$3C,$3C,$3F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $00,$00,$00,$03, $0C,$0C,$0C,$0F	dc.b $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $30,$30,$30,$33, $3C,$3C,$3C,$3F	dc.b $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $CC,$CC,$CC,$CF	dc.b $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $CC,$CC,$CC,$CF	dc.b $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $C0,$C0,$C0,$C3, $CC,$CC,$CC,$CF	dc.b $F0,$F0,$F0,$F3, $F0,$F0,$F0,$F3, $F0,$F0,$F0,$F3, $FC,$FC,$FC,$FF	EndP	END